{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/1351187-where-did-the-original-idea-for-aeon-s-aberrants-come-from","id":1351187,"title":"Where did the original idea for Aeon's aberrants come from?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: \u00c6on"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-26T18:06:19-08:00","id":1351187,"title":"Where did the original idea for Aeon's aberrants come from?","content":"So, I think Aeon is the only sci-fi setting I've ever seen where Earth used to have superheroes and then they became Lovecraftian space monsters. I assume it's because White Wolf wanted to do pulp/supers/sci-fi as a trilogy, but does anyone actually know what the thought process was there? Because it's weirdly original and somehow feels very plausible despite the clashing genre concepts. It's like if someone made a gothic punk monster-hunting modern setting set in the future of a Tolkien fantasy setting with the premise that all the halflings became the first vampires, and somehow it was awesome instead of absurd.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Is there any precedent in fiction for the whole &quot;future where superheroes became monsters?&quot; The closest I can think of are Marvel Zombies (sorta) and Brandon Sanderson's Reckoners series (more like the Aberrant War, though), but both of them came after Aeon, zombies aren't really like aberrants, and Reckoners might have actually been directly influenced by Aberrant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1711,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1711-zeea","user":"Zeea","date":". 11-26-2019, 09:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1711-zeea","name":"Zeea","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T22:06:44-08:00","id":1351225,"title":"","content":"Figure Watchmen is part of it. That work really broached the subject of superheroes having feet of clay. Some of it might have come from Frank Miller's The Dark Knight Returns. Still one occasional thread that's long been apart of superhero comics is the &quot;mirror universe&quot; concept. The place where in some other world our heroes are villains and the villains are the heroes. Lex Luthor is trying to save Earth from a psychopathic Superman, etc. The influences were there.<br /> <br /> Still, none of that takes away from the fact the developers created what they did","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2318-johntfs","name":"johntfs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"373"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T23:16:18-08:00","id":1351230,"title":"","content":"I do think the deconstruction is part of it, definitely. I also wonder where the &quot;the superheroes became space monsters in a sci-fi setting&quot; came from, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1711-zeea","name":"Zeea","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T01:06:41-08:00","id":1351237,"title":"","content":"Watchmen is right there... Doctor Mahattan is a Space Monster. X-men are probably also an influence. With the fairly constant wars of the future between Mutant and Human.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T02:13:08-08:00","id":1351245,"title":"","content":"Yeah. I think the thing I'm not conveying is the &quot;putting superheroes into the past of a space future sci-fi thing that isn't supers&quot; as being the weird part for me.<br /> <br /> Watchmen is still basically a superhero setting (even if a deconstructed one) and X-Men in the future is still superheroes. Reckoners is a weird setting but is still a modern supers setting.<br /> <br /> Aeon/Trinity is more of a traditional space sci-fi genre closer to Star Trek than Days of Future Past. I've seen plenty of superhero comics with grim futures, but I've never seen something like Star Trek or Babylon 5 have space monsters that used to be costumed superheroes showing up. Like, I can't imagine an episode of Star Trek where Data explains that this week's omnipotent space monster used to be a tights-wearing superhero named Spider-Man. None of the Shadows in Babylon 5 used to be Aquaman or Wonder Woman. Alien and Starship Troopers don't have Namor or Magneto in their backstories.<br /> <br /> I've never, ever seen fiction that's not about superheroes at all, doesn't have any traditional cape-and-tights-and-codenames superheroes in it, isn't marketed as superheroes, wouldn't be seen by the average person as &quot;superheroes,&quot; but has cape-and-tights superheroes as a major part of the backstory. Generally speaking, in any other setting I've seen where costumed superheroes exist in any era, the setting is about costumed superheroes and they exist in _every_ era. (Except Rifts, where you can technically play a superhero from another reality but they aren't really a big part of the setting and you can also technically play Zentraedi and Ninja Turtles so I wouldn't read too much into that.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1711-zeea","name":"Zeea","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T04:40:15-08:00","id":1351255,"title":"","content":"Zenith, a Grant Morrison comic from the late 80\u2019s, had superheroes transforming into Cthulhu-esque monsters as one of its main plot points. I can certainly see some influence there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3631-adamrice","name":"AdamRice","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T11:13:41-08:00","id":1351322,"title":"","content":"Honestly I would say the x-men futures and debately 2099 line in general probably were in part inspiration. That there is going to be a Post SUperhero age and its going to end badly has long been a halmark of Marvel. You can HEAVILY see alot of x-men inspiration especially in the Aberrant timeline. And there's been a long history of having crossovers between Star Trek comics and various normal comics with some how the &quot;X-men happens... then 400 years later Captain Kirk&quot; <br /> <br /> So I can see bits of the inspiration, but much like other things the Trinity Continuum put them together into a solid whole going from Mystery Men to Superheroes to Scifi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T13:23:20-08:00","id":1351350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zeea</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/1351187-where-did-the-original-idea-for-aeon-s-aberrants-come-from?p=1351245#post1351245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah. I think the thing I'm not conveying is the &quot;putting superheroes into the past of a space future sci-fi thing that isn't supers&quot; as being the weird part for me.<br /> <br /> Watchmen is still basically a superhero setting (even if a deconstructed one) and X-Men in the future is still superheroes. Reckoners is a weird setting but is still a modern supers setting.<br /> <br /> Aeon/Trinity is more of a traditional space sci-fi genre closer to Star Trek than Days of Future Past. I've seen plenty of superhero comics with grim futures, but I've never seen something like Star Trek or Babylon 5 have space monsters that used to be costumed superheroes showing up. Like, I can't imagine an episode of Star Trek where Data explains that this week's omnipotent space monster used to be a tights-wearing superhero named Spider-Man. None of the Shadows in Babylon 5 used to be Aquaman or Wonder Woman. Alien and Starship Troopers don't have Namor or Magneto in their backstories.<br /> <br /> I've never, ever seen fiction that's not about superheroes at all, doesn't have any traditional cape-and-tights-and-codenames superheroes in it, isn't marketed as superheroes, wouldn't be seen by the average person as &quot;superheroes,&quot; but has cape-and-tights superheroes as a major part of the backstory. Generally speaking, in any other setting I've seen where costumed superheroes exist in any era, the setting is about costumed superheroes and they exist in _every_ era. (Except Rifts, where you can technically play a superhero from another reality but they aren't really a big part of the setting and you can also technically play Zentraedi and Ninja Turtles so I wouldn't read too much into that.)</div> </div> </div> </div>The closest I've seen to that prior to Trinity was in Simon R. Green's Deathstalker series. One of the planet is dedicated to pleasure and filled with toys - including advanced robots as superheroes and supervillains. They all develop homicidal tendencies and try to murder anyone who lands on the planet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2318-johntfs","name":"johntfs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"373"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}